1
00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:01.634
In the previous lesson, you learned

2
00:00:01.634 --> 00:00:03.536
about the four pillars of agent governance.

3
00:00:03.803 --> 00:00:06.606
You can make the pillars actionable using Unity Catalog.

4
00:00:06.906 --> 00:00:08.775
In this lesson, you'll learn what

5
00:00:08.775 --> 00:00:10.910
Unity Catalog is and explore its components.

6
00:00:11.211 --> 00:00:12.812
All right, let's dive in.

7
00:00:13.046 --> 00:00:15.148
So as we saw from the first lecture,

8
00:00:15.148 --> 00:00:17.650
we have our governing agents life cycle

9
00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:19.953
where we prepare data, build agents,

10
00:00:19.953 --> 00:00:22.255
evaluate agents, deploy them,

11
00:00:22.255 --> 00:00:24.257
and then have this layer of governance.

12
00:00:24.557 --> 00:00:27.160
Now we'll see how we apply these layers of governance

13
00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:29.662
on Databricks with the help of Unity Catalog.

14
00:00:30.063 --> 00:00:33.566
Unity Catalog is essentially your governance foundation.

15
00:00:33.867 --> 00:00:36.469
Unity Catalog is a centralized data catalog

16
00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:39.072
that provides access control, auditing,

17
00:00:39.339 --> 00:00:42.042
lineage tracking, data quality monitoring,

18
00:00:42.142 --> 00:00:44.944
and more across Databricks workspaces.

19
00:00:45.545 --> 00:00:48.214
And since you'll be in a Databricks workspace.

20
00:00:48.214 --> 00:00:51.117
It's best to know that a Databricks workspace is an environment

21
00:00:51.117 --> 00:00:53.820
where teams write code, run notebooks, and access data.

22
00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:57.023
So it's a great way to work collaboratively with teammates.

23
00:00:57.223 --> 00:01:00.026
Unity Catalog provides this unified governance

24
00:01:00.026 --> 00:01:01.728
across all those workspaces.

25
00:01:02.395 --> 00:01:06.199
And when using Unity Catalog, it's best to understand the structure

26
00:01:06.199 --> 00:01:10.570
and the metastore. So the metastore is the top level container

27
00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:12.572
for data in Unity Catalog.

28
00:01:13.173 --> 00:01:17.177
And then metastores register metadata about data and AI assets

29
00:01:17.177 --> 00:01:20.180
and the permissions that govern access to them.

30
00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:23.283
Each metastore exposes a three-level namespace.

31
00:01:23.283 --> 00:01:26.286
So you have your Catalog, your Schema,

32
00:01:26.286 --> 00:01:28.888
and then your Table, Views,

33
00:01:28.955 --> 00:01:32.258
volumes, and functions by which your data can be organized.

34
00:01:32.459 --> 00:01:34.761
Now let's get into what each one of those means.

35
00:01:35.161 --> 00:01:37.163
So we have database objects.

36
00:01:37.764 --> 00:01:41.434
So Databricks primarily uses two primary securable objects

37
00:01:41.634 --> 00:01:43.436
to store and access your data.

38
00:01:43.736 --> 00:01:48.641
So you have your structured tabular data, which is in our tables.

39
00:01:49.042 --> 00:01:53.546
And then you have your unstructured, non-tabular data in volumes.

40
00:01:54.147 --> 00:01:58.017
So you can also create vector databases from both tables,

41
00:01:58.017 --> 00:02:00.320
so vector columns and volumes

42
00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:02.021
if you have a vector index

43
00:02:02.021 --> 00:02:04.023
using Databricks Vector Search.

44
00:02:04.524 --> 00:02:07.026
So these vector databases will still inherit

45
00:02:07.026 --> 00:02:09.028
all the Unity Catalog security

46
00:02:09.028 --> 00:02:12.499
and audit capabilities for all AI retrieval applications.

47
00:02:12.999 --> 00:02:15.301
Just to recap, you have your tables

48
00:02:15.301 --> 00:02:17.003
for your structured,

49
00:02:17.003 --> 00:02:18.705
your volumes for your non-structured,

50
00:02:18.705 --> 00:02:21.508
and with either one, you can create vector databases.

51
00:02:22.075 --> 00:02:25.912
Looking at Databricks objects from a Catalog, Schema,

52
00:02:25.912 --> 00:02:27.881
and Tables perspective.

53
00:02:27.881 --> 00:02:29.782
So that top level of the

54
00:02:29.782 --> 00:02:31.985
container is going to be the Catalog

55
00:02:31.985 --> 00:02:34.087
that contains all schemas.

56
00:02:34.087 --> 00:02:36.289
So you can have multiple schemas.

57
00:02:36.289 --> 00:02:39.993
Then you have your Schema, or often referred to as a database,

58
00:02:39.993 --> 00:02:42.695
and that contains all your data objects.

59
00:02:42.695 --> 00:02:45.064
So the data objects can be contained

60
00:02:45.632 --> 00:02:48.635
in a single schema. So these are your tables,

61
00:02:48.635 --> 00:02:51.638
collections of data organized by rows and columns.

62
00:02:52.038 --> 00:02:55.241
Views, a saved query against one or more tables.

63
00:02:55.441 --> 00:02:59.946
Volumes, again, all your unstructured data in a cloud object storage.

64
00:03:00.446 --> 00:03:02.849
Your volumes, which can be PDFs, they

65
00:03:02.849 --> 00:03:05.251
can be images, they can be videos,

66
00:03:05.652 --> 00:03:08.421
any unstructured data. Functions, so

67
00:03:08.788 --> 00:03:10.890
saved logic that returns values.

68
00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:14.294
Functions are also your tools or your skills,

69
00:03:14.294 --> 00:03:16.996
anything you can also attach to your agent

70
00:03:16.996 --> 00:03:18.798
to be able to query data.

71
00:03:18.798 --> 00:03:22.602
And then models. So these are AI models packaged with MLflow,

72
00:03:22.602 --> 00:03:24.604
and these are also your agents.

73
00:03:24.604 --> 00:03:27.307
So you can register your agent as an AI model

74
00:03:27.307 --> 00:03:29.809
that's packaged with MLflow.

75
00:03:29.809 --> 00:03:31.578
You'll see how to do that in lab two.

76
00:03:32.145 --> 00:03:34.347
In lab one of this course, you'll create

77
00:03:34.347 --> 00:03:37.951
and use Unity Catalog objects to create your tables.

78
00:03:37.951 --> 00:03:40.153
So we're going to be a company called Client Care,

79
00:03:40.153 --> 00:03:42.956
and we're going to have a database of all our HR data.

80
00:03:42.956 --> 00:03:45.658
So the schema is going to be called HR data.

81
00:03:45.658 --> 00:03:48.962
So remember, we have a catalog Client Care,

82
00:03:48.962 --> 00:03:53.962
schema HR data, and then we'll have tables of all

83
00:03:54.934 --> 00:03:58.705
that data, we'll create a view as an analyst view.

84
00:03:58.705 --> 00:04:02.108
So an analyst view will anonymize a lot of that key

85
00:04:02.108 --> 00:04:04.711
personal identifiable information.

86
00:04:04.711 --> 00:04:07.113
So social security numbers, it will anonymize,

87
00:04:07.113 --> 00:04:09.115
names, it will anonymize,

88
00:04:09.115 --> 00:04:10.783
and just give the information that

89
00:04:10.783 --> 00:04:12.719
would be needed for a data analyst.

90
00:04:12.719 --> 00:04:15.922
We're going to build functions. So we'll build masking functions

91
00:04:15.922 --> 00:04:18.958
and we'll build functions that can query the data.

92
00:04:18.958 --> 00:04:20.660
We're going to use that as a tool

93
00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:22.595
to bind to our agent so that our agent

94
00:04:22.595 --> 00:04:26.232
can query the data using that specific view we built.

95
00:04:26.499 --> 00:04:28.901
Models, so this is where we again, we register

96
00:04:28.901 --> 00:04:31.904
the model we're building as an MLflow model.

97
00:04:32.171 --> 00:04:34.073
And that's going to be the full agent.

98
00:04:34.073 --> 00:04:36.876
So we consider an agent as a model

99
00:04:36.876 --> 00:04:39.979
or any customized fine-tuned LLMs.

100
00:04:40.446 --> 00:04:43.349
Next you'll see how Unity Catalog provides centralized governance

101
00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:47.053
for all these data objects across your Databricks environment.

102
00:04:47.654 --> 00:04:50.256
And just before diving into lab one,

103
00:04:50.456 --> 00:04:52.859
when you have privilege presets,

104
00:04:52.959 --> 00:04:55.261
so we can grant all permissions,

105
00:04:55.361 --> 00:04:57.063
we can create manage permissions.

106
00:04:57.263 --> 00:04:59.866
You can have edit specific information,

107
00:04:59.966 --> 00:05:02.468
read permissions or custom permissions.

108
00:05:02.769 --> 00:05:04.771
We're going to have custom permissions for

109
00:05:04.771 --> 00:05:07.073
our developer group that we're going to create.

110
00:05:07.373 --> 00:05:09.542
And so this lab wants to use

111
00:05:09.709 --> 00:05:12.011
custom minimum specific access.

112
00:05:12.512 --> 00:05:16.015
So we're going to give these developers access to use the schema,

113
00:05:16.316 --> 00:05:19.218
create a model, create model versions,

114
00:05:19.218 --> 00:05:22.422
use SELECT so they can read data from tables and views,

115
00:05:22.622 --> 00:05:27.026
EXECUTE so that they can run functions and deployed models for inference,

116
00:05:27.226 --> 00:05:28.795
and then create tables so they

117
00:05:28.795 --> 00:05:30.630
can create new tables in the schema.

118
00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:32.965
So again, if you wanted to

119
00:05:32.965 --> 00:05:35.335
provide broad access to all your developers.

120
00:05:35.335 --> 00:05:37.637
It's as simple as clicking ALL PRIVILEGES.

121
00:05:37.637 --> 00:05:40.840
if you just want to manage access, you can manage access there.

122
00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:44.210
Custom presets, I can choose editor, reader,

123
00:05:44.210 --> 00:05:46.512
and it will change out the permissions.

124
00:05:46.512 --> 00:05:49.515
But Custom is going to let you have that hands-on approach.

125
00:05:49.515 --> 00:05:51.617
And of course, you can always revoke

126
00:05:51.617 --> 00:05:53.619
as easily as you grant access.
